Ember Court: a small multiplayer game that shows what the engine can do.

Players run around a walled court at sunset, collect embers for points and throw fire bolts at each other and at the sentinels
that patrol it. It plays offline, hosts a game for others to join, joins one, or runs as a dedicated server.

    ember_court                         play alone. A server can be started or joined later (F10, Windows > Network)
    ember_court --host [port]           play, and let other players join
    ember_court --connect address[:port]
    ember_court --server [port]         dedicated server: no window and no player of its own
    ember_court --connect address --bot      a computer controlled player, without a window. As many as you like

See src/game.h for the rest of the options (they are the same as gamecore_template's).

Controls:
    Mouse               look around. Left button: throw a fire bolt
    W A S D             walk
    Space               jump (high enough to get onto a crate)
    F10                 show the debug windows and free the mouse
    F11                 full screen
    Escape              quit

Scoring: an ember is 1 point, hitting a sentinel is 2, burning another player is 3. Green pickups heal.

THE MAP

The whole map is one prefab, "ember_court.map", in content/ember_court.gcpak. Nothing about the map is in the game's code: the
game registers its components and systems, calls gc::loadPrefab() once, and that is the world. The prefab has:

    the court           every wall, floor, ramp and crate: a RenderableComponent, and a ColliderComponent with the same mesh
    materials           one of every kind the renderer has: all three textures, a color texture only, no textures, see-through
                        glass (alpha blended), a grating with holes (alpha tested), and things that glow (emissive)
    the sky             a huge sphere seen from the inside, with an emissive texture
    lights              the sun (directional), the sky (ambient), and lamps and a fire (point lights), in lux and candela
    a camera            looks over the court when there is no player to look through
    baked shadows       a ShadowMapComponent: see below
    where things go     spawn points, pickup spawners and the sentinels' patrol routes. These are components of the game's own
                        (src/components.h), stored in the prefab by name like the engine's, and read by MatchSystem::readMap()
    a spinning beacon   a decoration with the game's SpinComponent on it

Nobody modelled it. It is made by a program, ember_court_mapgen (mapgen/main.cpp), which builds the prefab with gc::PrefabWriter
and makes the meshes, materials and two of the textures itself. The other textures are in content/textures.gcpak: assets are
found by name, whichever package file they are in.

The engine only draws shadows that were baked into a shadow map beforehand. gcpak_editor can bake one for any prefab that has a
directional light, so making the map takes two steps:

    ember_court_mapgen content/ember_court.gcpak
    gcpak_editor --bake-shadows ember_court.map content/ember_court.gcpak --no-cast sky --no-cast beacon

(The sky and the beacon are left out as the sky isn't really there, and a baked shadow can't turn with the beacon. See
tools/gcpak_editor/README.) The build does both steps: the ember_court_map target, which ember_court depends on. The map is made
again when the map generator or gcpak_editor changes. content/shaders.gcpak, textures.gcpak and meshes.gcpak have to be there
already, as for the other games (see tools/gcpak_editor/README).

To change the map, change mapgen/main.cpp and build. To see the prefab, open content/ember_court.gcpak in gcpak_editor.

WHAT IT SHOWS

    Prefabs             the map (above). Game components in a prefab: components.h
    Baked shadows       of everything in the map, including the holes in the grating. Players and other things that move are
                        shadowed by the map but cast no shadows themselves: the engine has no dynamic shadows
    Rendering           textured, plain, emissive, alpha tested and alpha blended materials. Meshes and materials that are made
                        when the game starts rather than loaded (createGameResources() in match.cpp). Automatic instancing
                        (the pillars and crates)
    Lights              a directional, an ambient and several point lights in the map, and point lights that move: every fire
                        bolt, pickup and sentinel has one. The camera's exposure is set to suit the evening
    Collisions          the CollisionSystem only answers questions, so the game has its own character controller on top of
                        its sphere queries (character.cpp). Ray casts stop fire bolts at walls (match.cpp) and tell bots what
                        they can see (bot.cpp). It all works without a window, on the server
    Replication         players are owned and moved by their clients, with health and scores decided by the server. Bolts,
                        pickups and sentinels are spawned and moved by the server, and interpolated on clients. See match.h
    Net events          asking to throw a bolt, being told where to respawn, hit markers (unreliable) and the kill feed
    The ECS             the game is components and systems registered with the World: game.cpp
    Debug UI            the HUD is drawn with ImGui. F10 shows the engine's own windows

Each host loads the map for itself, so the server never sends it. A dedicated server loads it too: it needs the colliders.

TESTING

    python scripts/test_replication.py --game ember_court

runs a server and several bots as separate processes, with simulated packet loss and latency, and checks that the bots see what
they should and that every process ends up with the same state. See notes/replication.txt.

WHAT ISN'T THERE

Bots don't plan routes, so they only go for pickups that are on their level, and get round things by trying another direction.
Clients are trusted to say where their own player is (see notes/replication.txt), so the server doesn't run the character
controller for them. Players don't block each other. The other players are all the same model, in different colors.
